00:34:08,275 --> 00:34:14,275
The Sound of Music  is a 1965 American musical drama film produced and
directed by Robert Wise from a screenplay written by Ernest Lehman.

12
00:37:14,482 --> 00:37:20,482
It is based on the 1959 stage musical composed by Richard Rodgers, with lyrics by Oscar Hammerstein II and a book by
Lindsay and Crouse, itself based on the 1949 memoir The Story of the Trapp Family Singers by Maria von Trapp.

13
00:40:20,689 --> 00:40:26,689
The film stars Julie Andrews and Christopher Plummer, with Richard
Haydn, Peggy Wood, Charmian Carr, and Eleanor Parker.

14
00:43:26,896 --> 00:43:32,896
Set in Salzburg, Austria, it is a fictional retelling of Maria von Trapp's experiences as governess to seven children, her
eventual marriage with their father Captain Georg von Trapp, and their escape during the Anschluss in 1938.

15
00:46:33,103 --> 00:46:39,103
Filming took place from March to September 1964 in Los Angeles and Salzburg.

16
00:49:39,310 --> 00:49:45,310
The Sound of Music was released by 20th Century Fox in the United States on
March 2, 1965, at first as a limited roadshow theatrical release.

17
00:52:45,517 --> 00:52:51,517
It initially received mixed reviews from film critics.

18
00:55:51,724 --> 00:55:57,724
However, the film was a major commercial success, becoming the number-one box
office film after four weeks, and the highest-grossing film of 1965.

19
00:58:57,931 --> 00:59:03,931
By November 1966, The Sound of Music had become the highest-grossing film in history,
surpassing Gone with the Wind, and it held that distinction for five years.

20
01:02:04,137 --> 01:02:10,137
The film was popular throughout the world, breaking
previous box-office records in 29 countries.

21
01:05:10,344 --> 01:05:16,344
The Sound of Music had an initial theatrical release that lasted
four and a half years, and two successful re-releases.

22
01:08:16,551 --> 01:08:22,551
It sold 283 million admissions worldwide and earned a
total worldwide gross of $286 million.

23
01:11:22,758 --> 01:11:28,758
The Sound of Music received five Academy Awards,
including Best Picture and Best Director.

24
01:14:28,965 --> 01:14:34,965
The film also received Golden Globe Awards for Best Motion Picture and Best Actress, the Directors Guild of America Award for
Outstanding Directorial Achievement, and the Writers Guild of America Award for Best Written American Musical.

25
01:17:35,172 --> 01:17:41,172
Since its original release, the film is widely regarded
as one of the greatest films of all time.

26
01:20:41,379 --> 01:20:47,379
In 1998, the American Film Institute (AFI) listed The Sound of Music as the 55th
greatest American film of all time, and the fourth-greatest film musical.

27
01:23:47,586 --> 01:23:53,586
In 2001, the United States Library of Congress selected the film for preservation in the National
Film Registry, finding it "culturally, historically, or aesthetically significant".

28
01:26:53,793 --> 01:26:59,793
The tone is intense.
